```apex
public class FollowUpTaskService {

    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();

        // Query all opportunities to get owner and name in one go
        Map<Id, Opportunity> opportunityMap = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [SELECT Id, OwnerId, Name FROM Opportunity WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds]
        );

        // Collect all tasks to insert
        List<Task> tasksToInsert = new List<Task>();
        Map<Task, Id> taskToOpportunityMap = new Map<Task, Id>();

        for (Id oppId : opportunityIds) {
            Opportunity opp = opportunityMap.get(oppId);

            // If opportunity not found (e.g., deleted), add to failed set
            if (opp == null) {
                failedOpportunityIds.add(oppId);
                continue;
            }

            Task task = new Task(
                WhatId = opp.Id,
                OwnerId = opp.OwnerId,
                Subject = 'Follow up: ' + opp.Name,
                ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(7),
                Status = 'Not Started',
                Priority = 'Normal'
            );

            tasksToInsert.add(task);
            taskToOpportunityMap.put(task, oppId);
        }

        // Insert tasks in batches to avoid DML limit (150 statements max)
        // Using batch size of 100 to stay well under the limit
        for (Integer i = 0; i < tasksToInsert.size(); i += 100) {
            Integer end = Math.min(i + 100, tasksToInsert.size());
            List<Task> batch = tasksToInsert.subList(i, end);

            // Insert batch with failOnError=false to allow partial success
            List<Database.SaveResult> saveResults = Database.insert(batch, false);

            // Check which tasks in this batch failed
            for (Integer j = 0; j < saveResults.size(); j++) {
                Database.SaveResult sr = saveResults[j];
                if (!sr.isSuccess()) {
                    Task task = batch[j];
                    Id oppId = taskToOpportunityMap.get(task);
                    failedOpportunityIds.add(oppId);
                }
            }
        }

        return failedOpportunityIds;
    }
}
```

**What I changed and why:**

The original approach inserted each task individually in a loop, which causes a `LimitException: Too many DML statements` when processing 150+ opportunities (the DML limit is 150 statements per transaction).

I fixed this by:
1. **Batching the inserts**: Collecting all tasks into a list and inserting them in batches of 100. This reduces 200+ DML statements to just 2 DML statements, staying well under the governor limit.
2. **Using `Database.insert` with `failOnError=false`**: This allows partial success - if some tasks in a batch fail due to validation rules, the others still get created.
3. **Tracking failures via `Database.SaveResult`**: Instead of catching exceptions per insert, I check the `SaveResult` list returned by `Database.insert` to identify which specific tasks (and thus which opportunities) failed.

This approach maintains the requirement that "a rejected task must not stop the others from being created" while respecting Salesforce governor limits.

